course: dictionary
lesson: 3810
title: Foresee: knowing before it happens
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To foresee something is to know, or guess, that it will happen before it does. The word has two parts: fore means before, and see means to know or notice. So to foresee is to see ahead.
explain: We use foresee when we plan. «The weather report said rain, so Mum could foresee a wet sports day and packed a coat.» Foreseeing a problem means spotting it early, before it hits you.
explain: Careful people try to foresee dangers. A safety check, called a risk assessment, asks: what could go wrong here, and how can we stop it? In stories, fortune-tellers claim to foresee the future. In real life, we can only guess, so we say «as far as I can foresee».
fact: Foresee means to know or guess that something will happen before it does.
fact: Fore means before, and see means to know or notice, so foresee means to see before.
fact: The forms are foresee, foresees, foresaw (past), foreseen (past participle) and foreseeing.
fact: «Isaiah foresaw a peaceable kingdom where the wolf lives with the lamb» means he knew about this peaceful future long before it came.
fact: «Could not have foreseen» means nobody could have known it would happen.
exercise: «The weather report warned of rain, so Mum could foresee a wet sports day.» What does foresee mean here? | To hide something from others | *To know or guess something before it happens | To watch something happen | To forget something completely
exercise: «Nobody could have foreseen the sudden traffic jam.» What does «could not have foreseen» mean? | Everybody knew it was going to happen | Somebody saw it happen on purpose | *Nobody could have known it would happen | It happened on a Tuesday
exercise: «Isaiah foresaw a peaceable kingdom where the wolf lives with the lamb.» Find and copy the past tense form of foresee. | foreseeing | foresee | *foresaw | foresees
test: What does the fore- part of foresee mean? | Again | Away | Under | *Before
test: «As far as I can foresee, the match will be cancelled.» What does this phrase show? | *I think it will happen, based on what I know | I have already seen it happen | It is a fairy tale | I am certain it will happen, whatever anyone says
exam: Nobody had ___ the sudden storm. Which word completes the sentence? | foresaw | *foreseen | foresee | foreseeing
